  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 324 AM EDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\n- Showers and storms this afternoon and overnight\n\nNow that we are at a closer forecast projection time to better \nleverage convection-allowing models (CAMs), a couple of differences \nare observed compared to this time yesterday. First, we see a \ngreater amount of forecast conditional instability, which is now \nsufficient for thunderstorms across most, if not all, of the \nforecast area. The 18/00Z HRRR, for example, indicates a ribbon of \nMUCAPE approaching 2000 J/kg immediately ahead of the cold front \nthat will traverse the forecast area tonight.\n\nThe second difference is that precipitation is now expected to exit \nthe forecast earlier than previously thought, thanks to a more \nprogressive cold front. Rain may even be totally out of the forecast \narea by sunrise Wednesday.\n\nBoth model guidance and satellite suggest stronger forcing aloft to \nhasten progress of the cold front. GOES full disk imagery shows a \nseasonably strong upper low entering the northern Ontario/southern \nHudson Bay region from Manitoba. A strong upper jet is diving \nsouthward over Saskatchewan on the back side of this low and this \nlikely will be the feature that that emerges as strong PV lobe \nrotating around the southern periphery of the low and crossing Upper \nMI and northern Lower MI early tonight.\n\nGreater conditional instability naturally raises the question of \nsevere potential with these thunderstorms. Several CAMs, both within \nthe SPC HREF suite and also external members such as RRFS and MPAS-\nNSSL, depict discrete cells with notable mid-level updraft helicity \nmoving across the northern forecast area early tonight; this speaks \nto the expected presence of marginally favorable deep layer vertical \nshear. Low level updraft helicity is much less pronounced, which is \nnot surprising given that the best low level shear should be \nrestricted to the poleward side of the front and away from the \nconvection.\n\nEstimated DCAPE will be respectable at 800+ J/kg, thanks in part to \nmoderately steep mid-level lapse rates approaching 8C/km. Thus, it \nis no surprise that wind will be the most plausible severe weather \ntype, as noted by SPC guidance across the west-northwest CWA. Still, \nwith the potential for discrete supercell structures depicted by the \nCAMs, we can't rule out some marginally severe hail given the \npotential for mesocyclones in this steep mid-level lapse rate \nenvironment. We are a bit less concerned about heavy rain given the \nanticipated more progressive nature of the front, but do note that \nWPC continues a marginal risk for excessive rainfall across far \nsouthwest Lower MI.\n\n\n- Precipitation chances return Friday\n\nNorthwest flow aloft will yield dry conditions Thursday. Broad upper \ntroughing across the northeastern U.S., including the Great Lakes, \nwill continue. Deterministic solutions show upper PV maxima working \ntheir way through this pattern during this time. The first one \narrives Friday, bringing a chance for showers and thunderstorms by \nFriday night. The weekend continues to look potentially unsettled \nwith a significant upper PV max diving southward and potentially \nevolving into a closed low through a deep layer of the troposphere \nsomewhere over the western Great Lakes.",
